The re-review found more problems, and most trace back to the `.partial`-file-then-rename scheme I added:
- error messages name a file the user never gave;
- long file names are refused;
- a user's own `.out.csv.partial` gets clobbered;
- two concurrent exports race;
- a symlink at the path is replaced instead of written through;
- the existing file's permissions are lost;
- a writable file in a read-only directory can no longer be exported.

Separately, an `Infinity` or `1E+30` total crashes with a traceback.

The simpler fix is to render the whole CSV in memory first and write it in one go. Every bad order then fails before the file is touched, and those seven problems no longer arise. The cost: a disk that fills up mid-write can still leave a short file, though the export exits 1 when it does. That narrows E6 to "an order that can't be exported leaves the previous file untouched". I'll put that amendment to you at the gate. Tests first:
